<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1578">1578</a>–<a href="/years/1637">1637</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, was the son of Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-archduke-of-austria">Charles II of Inner Austria</a> and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> before becoming emperor in 1619. A Jesuit-educated champion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, he expelled Protestant preachers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and his other lands. The Bohemian revolt against him in 1618 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and his victory at the White Mountain in 1620 was followed by forced re-Catholicisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ferdinand_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor_(Kunsthistorisches_Museum,_Wien).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born in the castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> on 9 July 1578, the son of Archduke Charles II and Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, her husband's niece, whose marriage reconciled the two leading Catholic families of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. His mother managed his education. In early 1590 he was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> in Bavaria, to keep him away from the Lutheran nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. His father died on 10 July 1590, and Ferdinand inherited Inner Austria, with regents appointed by his cousin Emperor Rudolf II. He took over its government in 1596 and 1597.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ferdinand was charged with defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He regarded the regulation of religion as a royal prerogative and from 1598 ordered the expulsion of Protestant pastors and teachers and set up commissions to restore Catholic parishes. His attempt to retake the fortress of Nagykanizsa ended in defeat in November 1601. In the Brothers' Quarrel he first backed Matthias against Rudolf II, but Matthias's concessions to Protestants outraged him. In 1617 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-spain">Philip III of Spain</a> acknowledged his right to succeed Matthias in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Hungary in return for territorial concessions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Bohemian estates confirmed Ferdinand as successor only after he promised to respect their privileges. Disputes over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letter-of-majesty">Letter of Majesty</a>, which set out the freedoms of Bohemian Protestants, led on 23 May 1618 to an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, when armed nobles threw two royal governors out of a window. After Matthias died on 20 March 1619, Ferdinand was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 28 August 1619, and the Bohemian estates deposed him as king and offered the crown to the Calvinist Frederick V of the Palatinate.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, which began in 1618, followed from the failures of Rudolf II and Matthias on one account, but Ferdinand's measures against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> spread it through the whole empire. He aimed to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> the only religion of the empire. The war left the empire devastated, and its population did not recover until 1710.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Ferdinand II</b> then granted it to Karl of Liechtenstein, whose family kept the ducal title thereafter.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krnov">Duchy of Krnov</a></li><li><blockquote>Both dukes were deposed in 1628 by <b>Emperor Ferdinand II</b>, who gave Mecklenburg to his general Albrecht von Wallenstein; Wallenstein governed from Güstrow until Swedish troops restored the dukes in 1631.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-gustrow">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1564 Graz was capital of Inner Austria under Archduke Charles II, who invited the Jesuits; his son <b>Ferdinand II</b> expelled Protestants.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>As a reward <b>Emperor Ferdinand II</b> transferred the electoral dignity of the Palatinate to him in 1623 and gave him the Upper Palatinate in 1628, gains confirmed at the Peace of Westphalia in 1648.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1619 <b>Archduke Ferdinand</b> became emperor as Ferdinand II, and his Inner Austrian lands merged into the united Habsburg Monarchy, though separate institutions in Graz survived until Maria Theresa's reforms in the 1740s.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1616" aria-label="Graz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4161 6004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4161 6004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1616">What was here in 1616? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
